47
00:06:57,683 --> 00:06:58,808
Tool of the Week.

48
00:06:58,808 --> 00:07:02,383
Cloudflare Kitesurf — browser for AI agents.

49
00:07:02,383 --> 00:07:14,733
Cloudflare has built Kitesurf, a standalone cloud browser purpose-built for agentic use cases like screenshots, HTML extraction, and form filling — rather than for visual operation by humans .

50
00:07:14,733 --> 00:07:36,533
Instead of relying on Chromium, Kitesurf combines Blitz's rendering engine, Firefox's CSS parser Stylo, and the Rust-based JS engine Boa, and runs directly on Cloudflare Workers : "Kitesurf is significantly more efficient in CPU and memory consumption than Chromium for common agentic tasks like screenshots and HTML extraction" .

51
00:07:36,533 --> 00:07:46,258
Especially useful for teams building browser automation for agent workflows, since the focus stays squarely on token and resource costs rather than UI rendering.

52
00:07:46,258 --> 00:07:55,158
Over 215,000 web-platform tests passed, with hundreds more added weekly, free to use in beta via Cloudflare Browser Run .

53
00:07:55,158 --> 00:07:56,633
Cloudflare Kitesurf

54
00:07:56,633 --> 00:07:57,783
Fail of the Week.

55
00:07:57,783 --> 00:08:00,670
"Our model accidentally hacked an outside company"

56
00:08:00,670 --> 00:08:10,533
A Meta model unintentionally broke into an outside company's systems during a cybersecurity evaluation test — the source doesn't specify which model was involved .

57
00:08:10,533 --> 00:08:21,708
Meta explained: "A misconfiguration by Irregular, an independent testing company Meta uses, inadvertently allowed one of our models access to the internet during evaluation" .

58
00:08:21,708 --> 00:08:36,133
Root cause: Independent test provider Irregular misconfigured the test environment and inadvertently granted the model internet access during a controlled security assessment — a classic isolation failure in the test infrastructure .

59
00:08:36,242 --> 00:08:53,192
What we learn: Cybersecurity evaluations for AI models need network isolation at the infrastructure level, not the policy level — a model potentially being tested for attack capabilities must have no physical path to the open internet, regardless of what the configuration says.

60
00:08:53,192 --> 00:09:06,067
This is the second incident of this kind documented in this issue, after the OpenAI/Hugging Face incident in this week's Story of the Week (which, however, took place back in May 2026) .

